# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Dataset/intendedUse node248
node248 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>intendedUse</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Dataset/intendedUse' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Dataset/intendedUse</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;**DEPRECATED in SPDX 3.1.**
Use /Core/intendedUse instead.

The intended use of a given dataset.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;**DEPRECATED in SPDX 3.1.**
Use /Core/intendedUse instead.

The intended use of a given dataset.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:note</td><td align='left'>&quot;**NOTE:**
This property is deprecated and only included for backward compatibility.
New documents should use
/Core/intendedUse
instead.

A free-form text that describes what the given dataset should be used for.

Some datasets are collected to be used only for particular purposes.

For example, medical data collected from a specific demography should only be applicable
for training machine learning models to make predictions for that demography.
In such a case, the intendedUse field would capture this information.
Similarly, if a dataset is collected for building a facial recognition model,
the intendedUse field would specify that.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibrn node249
node249 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>ibrn</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibrn' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibrn</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;International Business Registration Number (IBRN) &lt;https://ekys.org/business_identifiers/&gt; is a unique identifier for legal entities standardized by ISO 8000-116 &lt;https://www.iso.org/standard/75117.html&gt;. It is composed of two parts: a jurisdiction prefix and a local authoritative legal entity identifier (ALEI), separated by a colon. The prefix contains an ISO 3166 country code, an optional subdivision code, and a register code (e.g., US-DE.BER:3031657).&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;International Business Registration Number (IBRN) &lt;https://ekys.org/business_identifiers/&gt; is a unique identifier for legal entities standardized by ISO 8000-116 &lt;https://www.iso.org/standard/75117.html&gt;. It is composed of two parts: a jurisdiction prefix and a local authoritative legal entity identifier (ALEI), separated by a colon. The prefix contains an ISO 3166 country code, an optional subdivision code, and a register code (e.g., US-DE.BER:3031657).&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
